{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"brutal-dyable-lion","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# Cloud ADF codec (marklas), truly bidirectional\n\n## Context / Problem\nJira Cloud stores rich text as ADF (structured JSON). Today the reconciler pushes plain Markdown, so Cloud renders it as literal source. ADF is a structured, lossless target, so Cloud can be truly bidirectional. A hand-rolled mistune converter only reached 90.7% fixed-point over the corpus (Markdown emphasis serialization is the hard part), so we use a library.\n\n## Approach\nUse `marklas` (pure-Python, mistune-based, MIT) for Markdown↔ADF, behind an optional extra. **Vendor or fork-and-pin** it (single-maintainer bus-factor accepted but mitigated) at a sha-pinned version. Wire it into the existing Cloud backend (`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/backend.py`, which already defines `normalize_rich_text` at line 115) so outbound converts Markdown→ADF and inbound converts ADF→Markdown. Loop prevention keeps rebar's normalize-both-sides model: normalize each side through the same idempotent transform before diffing. Fix the one true 2-cycle oscillator — co-located `strong`+`strikethrough` (`**~~x~~**` ⇄ `~~**x**~~`, which marklas has no canonical order for) — with a deterministic mark-order canonicalizer applied to both sides. The ~18 transient whitespace canonicalizations (trailing whitespace; 3+ blank-line runs collapsing to 2) are accepted: they converge under normalize-both-sides.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/backend.py` — Markdown↔ADF conversion in the Cloud backend.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/inbound_fields.py` — shared `normalize_rich_text` + mark-order canonicalizer.\n- `pyproject.toml` — add the pinned `marklas` extra (or a vendored copy under `src/rebar/_vendor/marklas/`).\n- `tests/` — corpus idempotence + mark-order tests.\n\n## Testing\n- Happy path: a description with headings, a checklist, inline code, and a link round-trips Markdown→ADF→Markdown to a fixed point.\n- Oscillation regression: the two known combined-mark bodies converge in ≤1 pass after canonicalization; a property test over combined strong/strikethrough permutations asserts 100% convergence.\n- Corpus idempotence: over the 5,568 historical bodies, MD→ADF→MD reaches a fixed point for ≥99.6% and the residual is whitespace-only (transient, converges under normalize-both-sides).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `marklas` is vendored or forked and sha-pinned (no floating dependency on an upstream that could disappear); verified by the pin appearing in `pyproject.toml`/lock and an import test.\n- [ ] Cloud outbound emits ADF and inbound decodes ADF→Markdown; verified by `test_cloud_adf_roundtrip` on a structured body.\n- [ ] The combined strong+strikethrough oscillation is eliminated by a deterministic mark-order canonicalizer applied to both sides; verified by a regression test using the two known bodies plus a permutation property test (100% convergence).\n- [ ] Over the historical corpus, MD→ADF→MD is a fixed point for ≥99.6% of bodies with the remainder whitespace-only-transient; verified by `test_cloud_corpus_idempotence`.","id":"e59d-3418-67ff-4aff","parent_id":"708d-0767-1a51-4928","priority":2,"tags":["rich-text"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Cloud ADF codec (marklas), truly bidirectional"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785800285267189001,"uuid":"8e549a42-028e-4276-aa4a-a15b2840d37b"}